1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,511
You gave Ravi write access to
the work repository on Monday.

2
00:00:08,551 --> 00:00:13,844
On Wednesday his push, the upload of his
commits, still fails with 403, and Git

3
00:00:13,884 --> 00:00:19,312
never asks him for anything. No prompt,
no chance to type a different credential.

4
00:00:19,352 --> 00:00:20,595
Your CTO asks: why?

5
00:00:20,935 --> 00:00:26,072
Because his keychain, the Mac's password
store, holds a credential for another

6
00:00:26,112 --> 00:00:30,585
account. A 403 doesn't make Git
discard it, so Git keeps sending it.

7
00:00:30,625 --> 00:00:35,828
Granting access to his work account changed
nothing, because that account never

8
00:00:35,868 --> 00:00:37,288
arrives at the server.

9
00:00:37,628 --> 00:00:42,363
To see that in five minutes and not five
hours, you need to know exactly what Git

10
00:00:42,403 --> 00:00:46,667
does between "the server wants
authentication" and "the token is stored".

11
00:00:46,707 --> 00:00:50,322
Keep Ravi's Wednesday in mind.
You'll solve it before the end.

12
00:00:52,162 --> 00:00:55,954
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:55,994 --> 00:00:59,913
This is the first of five videos
on authentication, Chapter 16.

14
00:00:59,953 --> 00:01:03,934
The whole chapter rests on three
questions, asked in this order.

15
00:01:04,274 --> 00:01:09,687
Which transport and which server? That's
decided by the remote URL, after rewrites,

16
00:01:09,727 --> 00:01:14,681
and the evidence is git remote -v. Which
credential does the client present?

17
00:01:14,721 --> 00:01:20,200
Decided by Git's credential helpers for
HTTPS, and by ssh, its configuration and the

18
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:25,653
agent for SSH. And: who does the server say
you are, and what may that identity do?

19
00:01:25,693 --> 00:01:26,836
Decided by GitHub.

20
00:01:27,176 --> 00:01:31,841
A failure is always in exactly one of
the three. Commands copied from a search

21
00:01:31,881 --> 00:01:34,133
result usually change a different one.

22
00:01:34,473 --> 00:01:39,308
Today: the distinction between
authentication and authorization, the HTTPS

23
00:01:39,348 --> 00:01:44,709
transport, and the credential helper
protocol. The replays, credential-protocol.sh

24
00:01:44,749 --> 00:01:49,321
and credential-scope.sh, use a stand-in
helper and no real credential.

25
00:01:49,361 --> 00:01:52,746
Every token on screen is a
fake string from the lab.

26
00:01:52,786 --> 00:01:56,106
Then a walkthrough of Lab
20.2 in the normal shell.

27
00:01:56,146 --> 00:01:59,070
I never show a token,
and neither should you.

28
00:01:59,410 --> 00:02:05,004
Labels from the chapter's table: git
credential fill and gh auth status are 🟢 SAFE,

29
00:02:05,044 --> 00:02:10,569
though fill prints a secret on your screen.
git config set --global credential...,

30
00:02:10,609 --> 00:02:15,931
gh auth setup-git and gh auth login are
🟡 CAUTION: they change which credential

31
00:02:15,971 --> 00:02:21,225
answers, for every repository. git
credential reject is 🟡: it deletes a stored

32
00:02:21,265 --> 00:02:21,972
credential.

33
00:02:23,812 --> 00:02:25,224
After this video you can:

34
00:02:25,564 --> 00:02:28,453
Separate "who are you" from "what
may you do" in an error message.

35
00:02:28,793 --> 00:02:34,019
Describe what Git does from the server's
401 to the stored token, naming the helper

36
00:02:34,059 --> 00:02:34,717
operations.

37
00:02:35,057 --> 00:02:39,543
Find out which credential helper answers
for a host and where the token lives.

38
00:02:39,882 --> 00:02:42,360
Scope a helper to one host or one path.

39
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:44,130
Read a credential trace.

40
00:02:45,970 --> 00:02:50,656
In one sentence: authentication establishes
which account a connection belongs to,

41
00:02:50,696 --> 00:02:54,690
and authorization decides what that
account may do to this repository.

42
00:02:55,030 --> 00:03:00,218
Git has no accounts and no passwords. It
delegates the connection to a transport.

43
00:03:00,258 --> 00:03:05,381
Over HTTPS, Git speaks HTTP and attaches
a username and a secret obtained from a

44
00:03:05,421 --> 00:03:10,415
credential helper or a prompt. For GitHub
the secret is a token, a string that

45
00:03:10,455 --> 00:03:15,514
stands for an account with a subset of
its rights. Over SSH, Git starts the ssh

46
00:03:15,554 --> 00:03:20,290
program, which proves possession of a
private key. Git never sees the key.

47
00:03:20,630 --> 00:03:24,996
The server maps the token or key to
an account, which is authentication.

48
00:03:25,036 --> 00:03:29,463
Then it evaluates roles, token permissions
and organization rules for the

49
00:03:29,503 --> 00:03:31,605
repository, which is authorization.

50
00:03:31,945 --> 00:03:36,393
Three identities are involved in
everyday work, and they're independent.

51
00:03:36,433 --> 00:03:41,505
The commit identity: the author and committer
name and email written into commits,

52
00:03:41,545 --> 00:03:44,685
set by user.name and
user.email, checked by nobody.

53
00:03:44,725 --> 00:03:49,797
It's an assertion. The authentication
identity: the GitHub account that a token or

54
00:03:49,837 --> 00:03:53,412
SSH key belongs to, checked by
GitHub on every connection.

55
00:03:53,752 --> 00:03:57,198
The signing identity: the key
that signed a commit or tag.

56
00:03:57,238 --> 00:04:02,247
You can push commits written by anyone, as
any account that has Write, signed by any

57
00:04:02,287 --> 00:04:06,394
key or none. "It says Asha in the
log" is not evidence of who pushed.

58
00:04:06,734 --> 00:04:12,233
HTTPS. In one sentence: over HTTPS, GitHub
accepts a token in the place where HTTP

59
00:04:12,273 --> 00:04:17,839
expects a password, and nothing else. GitHub
has not accepted account passwords for

60
00:04:17,879 --> 00:04:23,175
Git since the thirteenth of August 2021.
Git still calls the secret a password,

61
00:04:23,215 --> 00:04:28,375
because to Git it is one: the prompt
reads "Password for", and you'll see it.

62
00:04:28,415 --> 00:04:33,779
In practice nobody should type a token:
the GitHub CLI or Git Credential Manager

63
00:04:33,819 --> 00:04:37,291
obtains one through the
browser and hands it to Git.

64
00:04:37,631 --> 00:04:42,728
One caveat the textbook marks unverified:
the exact text GitHub's server sends when

65
00:04:42,768 --> 00:04:47,865
a password is used is not quoted in any
GitHub documentation the course could find.

66
00:04:47,905 --> 00:04:53,064
Recognize the situation by Git's own last
line, fatal: Authentication failed for and

67
00:04:53,104 --> 00:04:55,168
the URL, which is in Git's source.

68
00:04:55,508 --> 00:05:00,267
Now the protocol. In one sentence: when a
server demands authentication, Git asks

69
00:05:00,307 --> 00:05:04,770
each configured credential helper in
turn, then falls back to prompting, and

70
00:05:04,810 --> 00:05:08,266
afterwards tells the helpers
whether the credential worked.

71
00:05:08,606 --> 00:05:13,684
A helper is a program that Git runs with
one argument, get, store or erase, and a

72
00:05:13,724 --> 00:05:18,992
description of the request on standard input
as key=value lines: protocol, host, and

73
00:05:19,032 --> 00:05:24,300
optionally path and username. git credential
fill, approve and reject drive the same

74
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:28,912
machinery by hand, so the whole exchange
can be watched without a server.

75
00:05:29,252 --> 00:05:35,317
Quick quiz, before the table. Git sends a
stored credential, and the server answers

76
00:05:35,357 --> 00:05:39,804
403. What does Git tell the
helper: store, erase, or nothing?

77
00:05:39,844 --> 00:05:40,980
Say it out loud.

78
00:05:45,820 --> 00:05:50,223
A request needs a credential:
the helper's store is read, get.

79
00:05:50,263 --> 00:05:53,950
The server accepted it: the
store is written, store.

80
00:05:53,990 --> 00:05:57,748
The server answered 401: the
entry is removed, erase.

81
00:05:57,788 --> 00:06:01,690
The server answered 403 or
404: the store is unchanged.

82
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:07,566
So the quiz answer is nothing. If you said
erase, you expected Git to tidy up, and

83
00:06:07,606 --> 00:06:10,146
that's a reasonable thing to expect.

84
00:06:10,486 --> 00:06:15,581
That last row is from Git's source: the
reject is called for a 401 with a credential

85
00:06:15,621 --> 00:06:20,717
that was sent, and for nothing else on this
path. In all four rows the working tree,

86
00:06:20,757 --> 00:06:24,751
the index, HEAD, the refs and the
Git configuration are unchanged.

87
00:06:25,091 --> 00:06:30,665
Which helper answers? credential.helper is
a list. Git asks each helper in order and

88
00:06:30,705 --> 00:06:34,541
stops at the first that returns
a username and a password.

89
00:06:34,581 --> 00:06:39,888
An empty value resets the list, which is
how a later configuration file discards

90
00:06:39,928 --> 00:06:45,501
helpers set by an earlier one.
credential.<URL>.helper and credential.<URL>.username

91
00:06:45,541 --> 00:06:50,914
apply only to matching URLs, and
credential.useHttpPath makes the repository path

92
00:06:50,954 --> 00:06:55,993
part of the lookup. By default a credential
is per host, not per repository.

93
00:06:56,333 --> 00:07:02,176
osxkeychain ships with Git on macOS and
keeps the secret in the login keychain.

94
00:07:02,216 --> 00:07:07,984
It stores whatever you typed. The GitHub
CLI configures itself as a helper for

95
00:07:08,024 --> 00:07:13,867
https://github.com, and keeps its token
in the system credential store, or in a

96
00:07:13,907 --> 00:07:16,622
plain text file if none is available.

97
00:07:16,962 --> 00:07:21,487
Git Credential Manager, value manager,
obtains tokens through the browser and is

98
00:07:21,527 --> 00:07:24,511
installed separately. store
writes a plain text file.

99
00:07:24,551 --> 00:07:29,018
The manual calls it discouraged, so don't
use it. cache keeps the secret in the

100
00:07:29,058 --> 00:07:33,640
memory of a background process and isn't
used in this course, because it starts a

101
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:34,039
daemon.

102
00:07:34,379 --> 00:07:39,524
Inside .git: nothing. A credential is never
stored in the repository unless you put

103
00:07:39,564 --> 00:07:44,395
it there. The helper list is configuration,
usually at system or global scope.

104
00:07:44,735 --> 00:07:49,833
And that explains a rule of this course:
labs/shell and the replays switch off the

105
00:07:49,873 --> 00:07:54,344
system configuration, where macOS
installations of Git usually configure

106
00:07:54,384 --> 00:07:59,231
osxkeychain. That's why the labs that
contact GitHub run in your normal shell.

107
00:07:59,271 --> 00:08:04,243
In the lab, Git finds no helper. In your
normal shell, it finds whichever one is

108
00:08:04,283 --> 00:08:04,932
configured.

109
00:08:06,772 --> 00:08:08,538
Two analogies from the textbook.

110
00:08:08,878 --> 00:08:11,869
For the distinction: a badge
reader and a door list.

111
00:08:11,909 --> 00:08:15,192
The reader checks that the
badge is real and whose it is.

112
00:08:15,232 --> 00:08:18,515
The list on each door says
which badge holders may enter.

113
00:08:18,555 --> 00:08:23,295
The analogy breaks because GitHub often
gives the same answer to both failures: an

114
00:08:23,335 --> 00:08:28,192
unknown badge and a known badge without
access can both get "there is no such door".

115
00:08:28,232 --> 00:08:31,515
The error text alone doesn't
tell you which check failed.

116
00:08:31,855 --> 00:08:34,987
For the protocol: a receptionist
with a key cabinet.

117
00:08:35,027 --> 00:08:38,647
Asked for a key, the receptionist
looks in the cabinet: get.

118
00:08:38,687 --> 00:08:43,648
If the visitor had to bring their own key
and it opened the door, it's hung in the

119
00:08:43,688 --> 00:08:48,589
cabinet: store. If a key from the cabinet
didn't open the door, it's thrown away:

120
00:08:48,629 --> 00:08:53,651
erase. The analogy breaks at the last step:
the receptionist throws a key away only

121
00:08:53,691 --> 00:08:58,470
when the door says "wrong key", 401, not
when it says "you may not enter", 403.

122
00:08:58,810 --> 00:09:01,468
That break is the hook.
It's Ravi's Wednesday.

123
00:09:03,308 --> 00:09:06,778
Git sends a request, and the
server demands authentication.

124
00:09:06,818 --> 00:09:10,169
Git asks the helper: get, with
the protocol and the host.

125
00:09:10,209 --> 00:09:14,868
The helper answers with a username and a
password, which for GitHub is a token.

126
00:09:14,908 --> 00:09:16,772
Git repeats the request with it.

127
00:09:17,112 --> 00:09:21,231
Then three outcomes. Accepted:
Git tells the helper store.

128
00:09:21,271 --> 00:09:26,824
A 401, wrong credential: Git tells the
helper erase, and next time it will ask

129
00:09:26,864 --> 00:09:30,553
again. A 403 or a 404: Git
tells the helper nothing.

130
00:09:30,593 --> 00:09:35,644
The wrong account stays in the cabinet
and answers every later request.

131
00:09:37,484 --> 00:09:42,872
No helper is configured in the sandbox,
and the lab forbids terminal prompts, as a

132
00:09:42,912 --> 00:09:45,056
CI job, an automated build, does.

133
00:09:45,396 --> 00:09:48,733
"Could not read Username ...
terminal prompts disabled".

134
00:09:48,773 --> 00:09:53,376
This is the error of every CI job that
reaches a private repository without a

135
00:09:53,416 --> 00:09:54,040
credential.

136
00:09:54,380 --> 00:09:58,626
A stand-in for the person at the keyboard
shows what Git would ask: two prompts, a

137
00:09:58,666 --> 00:09:59,986
username and a "password".

138
00:10:00,326 --> 00:10:05,711
The lab's helper, a short shell script.
Read its case statement: get prints what is

139
00:10:05,751 --> 00:10:11,202
stored, store writes it, erase deletes it.
It also logs every call with the password

140
00:10:11,242 --> 00:10:13,752
hidden. Do not use it for real secrets.

141
00:10:14,092 --> 00:10:19,068
Git asked the helper, get. The helper had
nothing. So Git fell through to the prompt

142
00:10:19,108 --> 00:10:20,323
that the lab forbids.

143
00:10:20,663 --> 00:10:25,147
What Git does after a server accepted a
credential that you typed: approve, and the

144
00:10:25,187 --> 00:10:26,073
helper stores it.

145
00:10:26,413 --> 00:10:31,522
The second request names a repository
path. Will the answer be specific to that

146
00:10:31,562 --> 00:10:34,064
repository? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

147
00:10:38,904 --> 00:10:43,432
The same credential. The path was dropped:
by default a credential is per host.

148
00:10:43,772 --> 00:10:48,477
GIT_TRACE=1 names the program that
answered. This line is the evidence for the

149
00:10:48,517 --> 00:10:50,302
second of the three questions.

150
00:10:50,642 --> 00:10:55,156
What Git does after a 401: reject,
and the stored credential is gone.

151
00:11:00,496 --> 00:11:05,356
The log is the protocol: get before a
request, store after success, erase after a

152
00:11:05,396 --> 00:11:06,566
rejected credential.

153
00:11:09,406 --> 00:11:14,189
Two helpers, two stored accounts. Which
account answers, and is the second helper

154
00:11:14,229 --> 00:11:16,272
asked at all? Make your prediction.

155
00:11:21,112 --> 00:11:24,138
The first helper answered and
the second was never asked.

156
00:11:24,178 --> 00:11:25,428
Order is the whole rule.

157
00:11:28,768 --> 00:11:33,629
An empty value resets the list for that
host, and what follows it replaces every

158
00:11:33,669 --> 00:11:38,469
helper configured before. Now github.com
gets the work account and another host

159
00:11:38,509 --> 00:11:40,184
still gets the personal one.

160
00:11:40,524 --> 00:11:45,915
The same reset for a single command, with
-c: no stored credential is read, changed

161
00:11:45,955 --> 00:11:46,569
or erased.

162
00:11:46,909 --> 00:11:51,949
With useHttpPath, the helper receives
path=, so it can keep one credential per

163
00:11:51,989 --> 00:11:56,116
repository. With a fixed username,
it is asked for that account.

164
00:11:56,456 --> 00:12:01,637
According to its help text, gh auth status
prints, per host, the active account,

165
00:12:01,677 --> 00:12:06,335
where its token is stored and the
token's scopes, with the token masked.

166
00:12:06,375 --> 00:12:11,816
The two git config commands tell you which
helpers are configured and in which file.

167
00:12:11,856 --> 00:12:17,036
If the second prints nothing, gh auth
setup-git configures the GitHub CLI as the

168
00:12:17,076 --> 00:12:18,472
helper for github.com.

169
00:12:18,812 --> 00:12:23,737
The textbook read what it writes from the
CLI's source and did not run it: first an

170
00:12:23,777 --> 00:12:27,925
empty value, to cut off helpers
configured elsewhere, then the gh auth

171
00:12:27,965 --> 00:12:32,531
git-credential helper. It is the
reset-then-add pattern you saw a minute ago.

172
00:12:32,871 --> 00:12:37,175
Try it now, thirty seconds, in your
normal shell, not the lab shell.

173
00:12:37,215 --> 00:12:42,222
Run the first git config line and read
two things: which helper, and which file

174
00:12:42,262 --> 00:12:43,500
names it. I'll wait.

175
00:12:48,340 --> 00:12:51,816
Whatever it printed, you now know
which program answers for you.

176
00:12:52,156 --> 00:12:57,050
The dry-run push sends nothing and shows
which program Git runs when a credential is

177
00:12:57,090 --> 00:13:00,163
needed. The last command
shows which account answers.

178
00:13:00,203 --> 00:13:03,394
The sed hides the secret
before it reaches your screen.

179
00:13:03,434 --> 00:13:06,448
Never run it without the
sed while sharing a screen.

180
00:13:08,288 --> 00:13:09,936
Five mistakes to watch for.

181
00:13:10,276 --> 00:13:14,307
Granting access and expecting a
failing push to start working.

182
00:13:14,347 --> 00:13:19,493
Root cause: a stored credential for another
account answers, and a 403 does not

183
00:13:19,533 --> 00:13:20,084
erase it.

184
00:13:20,424 --> 00:13:24,348
Typing the account password
at Git's "Password" prompt.

185
00:13:24,388 --> 00:13:29,249
Root cause: GitHub accepts only a
token there, since 13 August 2021.

186
00:13:29,589 --> 00:13:32,708
Treating the author in git
log as the person who pushed.

187
00:13:32,748 --> 00:13:37,051
Root cause: the commit identity is an
unchecked assertion; the authentication

188
00:13:37,091 --> 00:13:38,236
identity is separate.

189
00:13:38,576 --> 00:13:41,847
Adding a helper and seeing
the old account still answer.

190
00:13:41,887 --> 00:13:46,756
Root cause: Git stops at the first helper
that returns a credential; reset the list

191
00:13:46,796 --> 00:13:48,708
for the host with an empty value.

192
00:13:49,048 --> 00:13:53,882
Running a GitHub command inside labs/shell
and getting "could not read Username".

193
00:13:53,922 --> 00:13:58,936
Root cause: the lab shell switches off the
system configuration, where the helper is

194
00:13:58,976 --> 00:13:59,598
configured.

195
00:14:01,438 --> 00:14:06,556
Now, out of the lab. Ravi's Wednesday,
worked through the three questions.

196
00:14:06,596 --> 00:14:11,365
Transport and server: git remote -v
shows an HTTPS URL on github.com.

197
00:14:11,405 --> 00:14:17,150
Fine. Which credential does the client
present? GIT_TRACE=1 on a dry-run push names

198
00:14:17,190 --> 00:14:23,005
the helper, and gh auth status names the
account. It's his personal account: he once

199
00:14:23,045 --> 00:14:27,535
cloned a personal project over
HTTPS, and the keychain stored it.

200
00:14:27,575 --> 00:14:32,484
Who does the server say he is? A valid
account without permission: 403.

201
00:14:32,824 --> 00:14:37,681
So the failure is in the second question,
and the fix is there too: make the right

202
00:14:37,721 --> 00:14:42,220
account answer for that host, by resetting
the helper list for github.com or

203
00:14:42,260 --> 00:14:46,818
deleting the keychain entry. Nothing on
GitHub needed to change after Monday.

204
00:14:46,858 --> 00:14:51,477
The prevention, from the chapter's table:
one helper per host, set on purpose.

205
00:14:53,317 --> 00:14:58,951
Your turn. Do Lab 20.2, "HTTPS through the
GitHub CLI, and the helper behind it", in

206
00:14:58,991 --> 00:15:04,288
lab-manual/m20-authentication-ssh.md.
Part A runs in the lab shell with the toy

207
00:15:04,328 --> 00:15:09,625
helpers, and Part B runs in your normal
shell. Before each git credential fill,

208
00:15:09,665 --> 00:15:15,030
predict whether it will print a credential
or fail, and what line the helper log

209
00:15:15,070 --> 00:15:20,367
will gain. In Part B, predict which file
configures your helper before you ask.

210
00:15:20,707 --> 00:15:26,902
The challenge is Exercise 20.3,
"403, and Git never asks", in

211
00:15:26,942 --> 00:15:29,764
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

